WEBVTT

00:00:00.485 --> 00:00:03.187
PostgreSQL can subtract one timestamp from another.

00:00:03.187 --> 00:00:06.417
The result is an INTERVAL representing elapsed time.

00:00:06.417 --> 00:00:11.427
This is the direct way to answer questions such as length of stay or processing duration.

00:00:11.427 --> 00:00:19.562
If you later need a numeric number of hours or days, that is a separate conversion step.

00:00:19.562 --> 00:00:19.862
Choose temporal types based on meaning.

00:00:19.862 --> 00:00:22.862
DATE is for calendar dates without time of day.

00:00:22.862 --> 00:00:27.132
TIMESTAMP stores a date and time without a time-zone interpretation.

00:00:27.132 --> 00:00:33.982
TIMESTAMPTZ represents an instant in time and is usually safer for events that cross regions or

00:00:33.982 --> 00:00:35.600
daylight-saving changes.
